            "id": "pc1_s1_c2",
            "chapterNum": 2,
            "title": "Draft Audit Comments on Autonomous Bodies / PSUs & State Accounts",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: C&AG (DPC) Act 1971 Section 19 & 20; MSO (Audit) Section 7\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: DPC Act Section 19(2), 19(3), Section 20(1); Uniform Format of Accounts for CABs\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, verification criteria, and practical audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Separate Audit Report (SAR)**: Formal audit report on the Annual Accounts and Balance Sheet of Central/State Autonomous Bodies.\n• **Submission Deadline**: Audited accounts and SAR must be presented to Parliament within **9 months** of the close of financial year.\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **Form 25 Schedules**: Uniform Format of Balance Sheet, Income & Expenditure Account, and Receipts & Payments Account.\n• **Audit Checks**: Checking unspent grants-in-aid balances, depreciation calculation on assets created from govt grants, and actuarial liabilities under AS-15.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• **Statutory Exceptions**: Where audit of autonomous body is not mandated by Act of Parliament, audit can be undertaken under Section 20(1) only upon request of the President/Governor after consulting CAG.\n• Audit fees charged for non-commercial bodies as per standard IA&AD scales.\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **SAR Case Scenario**: An Autonomous Body credited Government Grants of ₹15.00 Crore directly to Capital Reserve instead of Income & Expenditure Account without creating physical capital assets.\n• **Model SAR Comment**: Non-compliance with AS-12 / IGAS-2 resulting in understatement of revenue deficit by ₹15.00 Crore.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### Draft Audit Comments on Separate Audit Reports (SAR) of Autonomous Bodies & PSUs\n\n#### 1. Statutory Mandate for Audit of Autonomous Bodies\nUnder Section 19(2) and Section 20(1) of the Comptroller and Auditor General's (DPC) Act, 1971, the C&AG is the sole auditor of Central and State Autonomous Bodies (universities, research councils, port trusts, statutory boards).\n- The C&AG certifies the annual accounts (Balance Sheet, Income & Expenditure Account, Receipts & Payments Account) and issues the **Separate Audit Report (SAR)**.\n- **Audit Opinion Matrix**:\n  - *Unqualified (Clean) Opinion*: Accounts present a True and Fair view in all material respects.\n  - *Qualified Opinion*: Accounts present a True and Fair view subject to specific, quantified distortions stated in audit comments.\n  - *Adverse Opinion*: Misstatements are both material and pervasive; accounts do not present a True and Fair view.\n  - *Disclaimer of Opinion*: Inability to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence; scope limitation.\n\n#### 2. Key Audit Examination Areas for Draft Audit Comments\n1. **Understatement / Overstatement of Fixed Assets & Depreciation**:\n   - Failure to provide depreciation on fixed assets under Schedule 8 in accordance with prescribed rates, leading to overstatement of fixed assets and understatement of deficit.\n   - Non-capitalization of Capital Work-in-Progress (CWIP) despite buildings/equipment being taken into active operational use.\n2. **Incorrect Treatment of Government Grants**:\n   - Crediting unspent operational grants directly to Income, artificially inflating operational surplus.\n   - Treating capital grants as operational income rather than crediting to Corpus/Capital Fund.\n3. **Non-Provision of Retirement Liabilities (AS-15 Violation)**:\n   - Recognizing gratuity and leave encashment on cash basis instead of creating provisions based on **Actuarial Valuation** under Accounting Standard 15.\n4. **Omission of Contingent Liabilities (Schedule 25)**:\n   - Failure to disclose pending legal suits, arbitration claims, or disputed municipal tax demands.\n\n#### 3. Standard Draft Audit Comment Format (Model Exam Draft)\n**Draft Comment on Balance Sheet - Fixed Assets (Schedule 8)**:\n*\"1. Overstatement of Fixed Assets and Understatement of Deficit by ₹12.40 Crore:*\n\nThe National Water Development Agency (NWDA) completed construction of its Regional Headquarters Building at Hyderabad and took physical occupancy in April 2021. However, the expenditure of ₹12.40 Crore continued to be reflected under **Capital Work-in-Progress (Schedule 8)** up to 31st March 2023. Failure to capitalize the completed building resulted in non-charging of depreciation at 5% per annum for two years (₹1.21 Crore). Consequently, Fixed Assets were overstated by ₹1.21 Crore, and the Deficit for the year was understated by ₹1.21 Crore.\"*\n\n**Draft Comment on Income and Expenditure Account - Government Grants (Schedule 13)**:\n*\"2. Overstatement of Income and Understatement of Current Liabilities by ₹28.50 Crore:*\n\nDuring FY 2022-23, the Council received Grants-in-Aid of ₹100.00 Crore from the Ministry of Science and Technology for research projects. Out of this, only ₹71.50 Crore was actually expended during the year. Contrary to the Uniform Format of Accounts and GFR Rule 230(8), the Council recognized the entire grant of ₹100.00 Crore as operational income in the Income & Expenditure Account. The unspent grant balance of ₹28.50 Crore ought to have been reflected as a liability under **Current Liabilities (Schedule 7)**. This has resulted in overstatement of Income and Net Surplus by ₹28.50 Crore and understatement of Current Liabilities by ₹28.50 Crore.\"*",
