            "chapterNum": 1,
            "title": "Precis Writing: Codal Rules, Length Calculation & Objective Summary Mechanics",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: IA&AD Style Guide (Published by Authority of C&AG of India) & Manual of Office Procedure\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: IA&AD Style Guide Chapter 4; MOP Paras on Summary Records and Briefing Notes\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, verification criteria, and practical audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Length Standard**: Exactly **one-third (1/3rd)** of the original text length (permissible margin ±5 words).\n• **Mandatory Header**: Title reflecting core theme and explicit Word Count statement at bottom (e.g., 'Original Words: 450 | Precis Words: 150').\n• **Voice & Tense**: Written strictly in third person and past/neutral reporting tense.\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **3-Pass Reading Procedure**:\n  1. First Pass: Skim for central theme.\n  2. Second Pass: Underline core arguments, delete statistical trivia and metaphors.\n  3. Third Pass: Draft coherent precis using own words, ensuring logical connectivity using transitions.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• No personal opinions, external knowledge, or rhetorical questions allowed.\n• Do not copy verbatim sentences from the original passage.\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Practical Exercise**: Summarize a 420-word CAG audit observation on irregular mobilization advance granted without interest.\n• **Model Precis**: Precis must be 140 words, capturing: contractor selection defect, advance release violating CPWD Manual Para 32.4, absence of bank guarantee, and resultant loss of ₹3.50 Crore.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### Précis Writing: Codal Rules, Word Length Calculation & Objective Summary Mechanics\n\n#### 1. Core Principles & Examination Rules\nA Précis (derived from French for *precise*) is a clear, concise, objective summary of the essential ideas of a comprehensive passage, written in the candidate's own words.\n- **Golden Rules of Précis Writing**:\n  1. **Strict Length Requirement**: A précis must be **exactly one-third ($\\pm 5\\%$)** of the length of the original passage. If the passage is 450 words, the précis must contain 140 to 160 words.\n  2. **Mandatory Title**: Every précis must have an appropriate, catchy, and comprehensive **Title** that captures the central theme.\n  3. **Own Words**: Do not lift sentences directly from the passage. Paraphrase ideas using concise administrative vocabulary.\n  4. **Third Person & Past Tense**: Always write in the third person (*\"The author asserts...\", \"The commission observed...\"*) and indirect speech.\n  5. **No Personal Opinions**: Do not add external facts, personal views, critiques, or illustrations not present in the original text.\n  6. **Eliminate Non-Essentials**: Strip away rhetorical flourishes, repetitions, quotations, detailed statistics, metaphors, and digressions.\n\n#### 2. Step-by-Step Writing Methodology\n- **Step 1 (First Reading)**: Read the passage rapidly to identify the central theme and formulate a tentative Title.\n- **Step 2 (Second Reading)**: Read closely with a pencil; underline the essential topic sentence of each paragraph and cross out ornamental text.\n- **Step 3 (Count Words)**: Count the exact words in the passage ($W_{orig}$); divide by 3 to find your target count ($W_{target} = W_{orig} / 3$).\n- **Step 4 (Drafting Rough Précis)**: Assemble the core ideas into a cohesive, logical paragraph using transition words (*However, Consequently, Furthermore*). Count the rough draft words.\n- **Step 5 (Final Refinement)**: Polish for conciseness, ensure smooth flow, and write the final précis.\n- **Mandatory Word Count Note at Bottom**:\n  - *Words in Original Passage*: 450\n  - *Words in Précis*: 150",
