            "chapterNum": 2,
            "title": "Ethics in Audit, including CAG's Code of Ethics and ISSAI 130",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: CAG's Code of Ethics & ISSAI 130 (Code of Ethics for Supreme Audit Institutions)\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: ISSAI 130 Core Values: 1. Integrity, 2. Independence & Objectivity, 3. Competence, 4. Professional Behavior, 5. Confidentiality and Transparency\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, verification criteria, and practical audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Conflict of Interest**: Mandatory declaration of conflict before commencing audit of any entity.\n• **Cooling-Off Period**: Minimum **2 years** restriction before auditing an office where the auditor was previously posted or has close relatives.\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **Code of Ethics Declaration**: Annual written undertaking submitted by all IA&AD officers and staff before commencement of audit tour.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• Auditors shall not solicit or accept gifts, hospitality, or personal favors from any audited entity (strict bar under CCS Conduct Rules & ISSAI 130).\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Ethical Dilemma Scenario**: An audit officer accepts free five-star hotel lodging and transport arranged by a vendor of the auditee department during field inspection.\n• **Violation & Disciplinary Action**: Severe breach of ISSAI 130 Integrity & Independence; liable to major disciplinary penalty under CCS (CCA) Rules 1965.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### Ethics in Public Audit: The C&AG’s Code of Ethics & ISSAI 130\n\n#### 1. Concept and Importance of Professional Ethics in Audit\nBecause auditors in the Indian Audit & Accounts Department have statutory access to highly confidential state documents, sensitive procurement data, and executive files, maintaining the highest ethical standards is paramount to ensure public trust and credibility.\n- **Governing Codes**:\n  - *CAG's Code of Ethics (notified under Regulations on Audit and Accounts)*.\n  - *ISSAI 130 (INTOSAI Code of Ethics)*.\n  - *Central Civil Services (Conduct) Rules, 1964*.\n\n#### 2. The Five Fundamental Ethical Principles (ISSAI 130)\n1. **Integrity**:\n   - Auditors must act honestly, reliably, in good faith, and in the public interest.\n   - Refuse any gifts, hospitality, favors, or entertainment from auditee entities.\n2. **Independence and Objectivity**:\n   - Maintain freedom from political influence, administrative pressure, or personal bias.\n   - *Conflict of Interest Declaration*: If an auditor has a personal, familial, or financial relationship with an auditee or held an administrative post in that entity within the preceding two years, they must formally declare the conflict and recuse themselves.\n3. **Competence**:\n   - Acquire and maintain professional knowledge, analytical skills, and codal mastery through continuous professional education.\n   - Exercise due professional care and diligence in gathering and evaluating evidence.\n4. **Professional Behavior**:\n   - Comply with all applicable laws, auditing standards, and departmental instructions.\n   - Conduct entry/exit conferences and field inspections with courtesy, professionalism, and firmness.\n5. **Confidentiality and Transparency**:\n   - Protect confidential and privileged information acquired during audit; do not disclose it to the media, private parties, or unauthorized persons prior to the laying of the Audit Report before Parliament/Legislature.",
            "gist": "CAG Code of Ethics (ISSAI 130) mandates Integrity, Independence/Objectivity, Professional Competence, Confidentiality, and Professional Behaviour.",
            "crux": "1. Independence in fact and appearance.\n2. Zero tolerance for gifts or conflicts of interest.\n3. Confidentiality until reports are placed before Legislature.\n4. Exercising due professional care and diligence.",
