            "id": "pc2_s4_c1",
            "chapterNum": 1,
            "title": "Environment audit (Environment and Climate Change Auditing Guidelines)",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: CAG's Guidelines on Environment and Climate Change Auditing\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: Environment Protection Act 1986; Water Act 1974; Air Act 1981; INTOSAI WGEA Guidelines\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, verification criteria, and practical audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Scope**: Audit of environmental programs, pollution control boards, afforestation funds (CAMPA), biodiversity conservation, and renewable energy targets.\n• **Key Audit Criteria**: National Clean Air Programme (NCAP) standards, National Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC), SDG Goals 6, 13, 14, 15.\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **Environmental Performance Matrices**: Baseline data vs. actual air/water quality parameters; Physical GIS/satellite verification of compensatory afforestation.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• Environmental compliance cannot be relaxed on grounds of commercial profitability of public enterprises.\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Environment Audit Case**: State CAMPA fund received ₹450 Crore for compensatory afforestation; ₹120 Crore spent on renovation of forest officers' rest houses and luxury SUVs.\n• **Audit Finding**: Diversion of compensatory afforestation funds violating Supreme Court orders and CAF Act 2016.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### Environment audit (Environment and Climate Change Auditing Guidelines)\n\nEnvironmental degradation, resource depletion, and climate change have elevated **Environment Audit** into a specialized priority within the **Comptroller and Auditor General of India (CAG)**. Operating under the international standards of the **International Organization of Supreme Audit Institutions (INTOSAI)** and national environmental statutes, CAG audits evaluate government compliance, resource allocation, and program performance in ecological preservation and climate action.\n\n---\n\n### 1. International Framework: INTOSAI P-70 and WGEA\n- **INTOSAI Working Group on Environmental Auditing (WGEA)**: The global standard-setting body for supreme audit institutions (SAIs) in environmental auditing. CAG of India is an active, prominent member.\n- **INTOSAI P-70 (formerly ISSAI 5110)**: *Environmental Auditing Guidelines for Supreme Audit Institutions*. Defines environmental auditing as auditing environmental management systems, adherence to environmental laws, and evaluating the ecological impact of government initiatives.\n- **Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)**: Integration of UN Agenda 2030 into environmental audit planning, specifically:\n  - **SDG 6**: Clean Water and Sanitation.\n  - **SDG 13**: Climate Action.\n  - **SDG 14**: Life Below Water.\n  - **SDG 15**: Life on Land.\n  - **SDG 7**: Affordable and Clean Energy.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Typology of Environmental Audits\nEnvironmental audit within IA&AD is applied across three primary audit methodologies:\n\n```\n                    TYPOLOGY OF ENVIRONMENTAL AUDIT\n                                   |\n         +-------------------------+-------------------------+\n         |                                                   |\n   COMPLIANCE AUDIT                  FINANCIAL AUDIT               PERFORMANCE AUDIT\n   - Air, Water, Forest Acts        - Environmental liabilities   - Namami Gange, NCAP, CAMPA\n   - EIA notifications & EC rules   - Green taxes & cesses        - Renewable Energy targets\n   - Waste management compliance    - Restoration provisions      - 3Es: Economy, Efficiency,\n   - Effluent discharge standards   - Clean Energy Fund spend       Effectiveness of policies\n```\n\n1. **Compliance Audit**:\n   - Assesses adherence to environmental statutory mandates, effluent discharge norms, and ambient air standards.\n   - Auditing compliance with statutory conditions attached to **Environmental Clearances (EC)** and **Forest Clearances (FC)** granted by the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change (MoEFCC).\n2. **Financial Audit**:\n   - Examines environmental liabilities, asset retirement obligations, provisions for environmental remediation in PSU accounts, and tracking dedicated environmental funds (e.g., **CAMPA** - Compensatory Afforestation Fund Management and Planning Authority; National Clean Energy Fund).\n3. **Performance Audit**:\n   - Evaluates the **Economy, Efficiency, and Effectiveness (3Es)** of major national environmental programs.\n   - Landmark CAG Performance Audits include:\n     - Performance Audit on *Rejuvenation of River Ganga (Namami Gange)*.\n     - Audit on *Groundwater Management and Regulation in India*.\n     - Audit on *Conservation of Coastal Ecosystems (CRZ notifications)*.\n     - Audit on *National Clean Air Programme (NCAP)*.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Indian Environmental Statutes (Audit Criteria)\nAudit observations must be grounded in specific statutory requirements:\n- **Environment (Protection) Act, 1986**: Umbrella legislation empowering the Central Government to protect environmental quality and regulate hazardous industries.\n- **Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974**: Mandates establishment of CPCB and SPCBs, Consent to Establish (CTE), and Consent to Operate (CTO).\n- **Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981**: Controls industrial emissions and defines air pollution control areas.\n- **Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980**: Restricts de-reservation of forests or diversion of forest land for non-forest purposes without prior Central approval.\n- **Biological Diversity Act, 2002**: Protection of biological resources and fair sharing of benefits.\n- **National Green Tribunal (NGT) Act, 2010**: Enforcing orders and compliance with polluter-pays principles.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Methodological Tools and Institutional Support\n- **Geographic Information Systems (GIS) and Satellite Remote Sensing**:\n  - Extensive deployment of satellite imagery (from ISRO/NRSC) to independently verify afforestation claims under CAMPA, detect illegal mining, track coastal zone encroachments, and assess forest canopy cover changes.\n- **CAG's iCED (International Centre for Environment Audit and Sustainable Development)**:\n  - Located in Jaipur, Rajasthan, **iCED** is CAG's global centre of excellence for environment audit, certified as a Green Building and serving as the global training facility for INTOSAI WGEA.\n",
            "gist": "Environment audit examines statutory compliance, pollution control, forest preservation, climate change programs, and SDG implementation.",
            "crux": "1. Evaluates compliance with environmental clearances and EIA notifications.\n2. Audits waste management and conservation funds (CAMPA).\n3. Employs GIS and satellite imagery as audit evidence.",
            "flashcards": [
              {
                "term": "Environment audit (Environment and Clima",
                "def": "Environment audit examines statutory compliance, pollution control, forest preservation, climate change programs, and SD"
              }
            ],
            "sourceDoc": "Environment & Climate Change Auditing Guidelines"
