            "id": "pc3_s1_c3",
            "chapterNum": 3,
            "title": "1.3 Computer Networks: LAN/WAN/Internet and cloud computing concepts (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS)",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: MeitY Cloud First Policy & Guidelines for Cloud Service Providers (CSPs)\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: MeitY Empanelment Guidelines for MeghRaj Cloud; IT Audit Manual 2024 Chapter 6\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, verification criteria, and practical audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Network Topology**: LAN, WAN, VPN with AES-256 encryption; MPLS leased lines for treasury networks.\n• **Cloud Delivery Models**: IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service), PaaS (Platform as a Service), SaaS (Software as a Service).\n• **Cloud Deployment Models**: Public, Private, Hybrid, Community (MeghRaj Government Cloud).\n• **Uptime SLA Ceiling**: Minimum 99.9% uptime prescribed for critical government treasury portals.\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **SLA Monitoring Log**, Network Diagram Register, Bandwidth Utilization Schedule, Firewall Rule Register.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• Storing sensitive government or citizen personal data in cloud servers located outside India is prohibited under DPDP Act 2023 and MeitY policy.\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Audit Case Study**: Treasury IFMS hosted on third-party cloud experienced 142 hours of unmonitored outage at financial year-end.\n• **Audit Finding**: Failure to enforce penalty clauses in CSP Service Level Agreement resulting in loss of ₹35 Lakhs SLA penalties.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### 1.3 Computer Networks & Cloud Computing: Architectures, Protocols & Service Models\n\n#### 1. Computer Network Topologies and Architectures\nA computer network is an interconnected collection of autonomous computing nodes that exchange data over shared physical or wireless transmission media.\n- **Geographical Network Classification**:\n  - *LAN (Local Area Network)*: Restricted geographical area (office room, building, campus) operating at high bandwidth (1 Gbps - 10 Gbps) over Ethernet UTP cables or Wi-Fi.\n  - *WAN (Wide Area Network)*: Spans vast geographical zones across cities, states, or continents (e.g. the Internet, or State Wide Area Networks - SWAN connecting all district treasuries to state capitals).\n- **Physical Topologies**:\n  - *Star Topology*: Most common modern enterprise topology. All nodes connect independently to a central switch/hub. Failure of a single cable does not affect other nodes.\n  - *Mesh Topology*: Every node connected directly to every other node. Offers maximum redundancy and fault tolerance; used in mission-critical sovereign data centers.\n\n#### 2. The 7-Layer OSI Model vs. 4-Layer TCP/IP Protocol Stack\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                     OSI 7-LAYER MODEL VS. TCP/IP PROTOCOL SUITE                   |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| OSI LAYER              | PROTOCOL EXAMPLES       | CORE AUDIT & SECURITY FUNCTION |\n| 7. Application Layer   | HTTPS, SFTP, DNS, SMTP  | User interface, encryption     |\n| 6. Presentation Layer  | SSL/TLS, ASCII, JPEG    | Data formatting, cryptography  |\n| 5. Session Layer       | NetBIOS, RPC, Sockets   | Session setup, checkpoints     |\n| 4. Transport Layer     | TCP (Reliable), UDP     | Port numbers (443, 80), flows  |\n| 3. Network Layer       | IP (IPv4 / IPv6), ICMP  | IP Routing, packets, routers   |\n| 2. Data Link Layer     | Ethernet (802.3), Wi-Fi | MAC addressing, network switch |\n| 1. Physical Layer      | Fiber Optic, Cat6 Cable | Binary bit transmission        |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n#### 3. Cloud Computing: Service Models and Deployment Architectures\nCloud computing delivers on-demand computing services (servers, storage, databases, networking, software) over the Internet under a pay-as-you-go commercial model.\n- **The Three Core Cloud Service Models (SPI Framework)**:\n  1. **Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)**:\n     - The cloud provider delivers raw physical or virtualized hardware (virtual machines, storage, networks, operating systems).\n     - *Customer Responsibility*: Manages the OS, runtime, middleware, data, and applications.\n     - *Examples*: Amazon EC2, Microsoft Azure VMs, Google Compute Engine, National Cloud (MeghRaj by NIC).\n  2. **Platform as a Service (PaaS)**:\n     - The provider delivers a pre-configured hardware and software development environment (operating system, web server, database engine).\n     - *Customer Responsibility*: Develops and deploys application code and data.\n     - *Examples*: AWS Elastic Beanstalk, Google App Engine, Azure SQL Database.\n  3. **Software as a Service (SaaS)**:\n     - The provider delivers fully managed, end-user application software accessible via a web browser.\n     - *Customer Responsibility*: Manages user access and operational data.\n     - *Examples*: Microsoft 365, Google Workspace, GeM Portal, PFMS, e-Office.\n- **Cloud Deployment Models**:\n  - *Public Cloud*: Shared multi-tenant infrastructure owned and operated by a commercial third party (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud).\n  - *Private Cloud*: Infrastructure operated exclusively for a single enterprise or sovereign government entity (e.g. MeghRaj GI Cloud).\n  - *Hybrid Cloud*: Integrated combination of private and public clouds with data and application portability.",
            "gist": "Networks connect systems via LAN/WAN using TCP/IP. Cloud models include IaaS (raw compute/storage), PaaS (application runtime), and SaaS (complete web applications).",
            "crux": "1. MeghRaj is Government of India's GI Cloud.\n2. IaaS gives maximum control; SaaS provides ready-to-use software.\n3. Network audit examines firewall rules, VPN encryption, and access controls.",
