            "id": "pc3_s1_c5",
            "chapterNum": 5,
            "title": "1.5 AI in Auditing: Leverage for data analysis, public LLM precautions, Responsible AI, risks in government AI systems",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: CAG Strategic Framework for Artificial Intelligence (2024 Edition)\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: CAG Strategic Framework Paras 1 to 15; MeitY Advisory on AI Deployment\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, verification criteria, and practical audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Public LLM Restriction**: Inputting classified government files, draft audit paragraphs, or confidential audit findings into public LLMs (e.g., ChatGPT, Claude) is strictly prohibited.\n• **Responsible AI Pillars**: Accountability, Transparency, Fairness, Data Privacy, Human-in-the-loop oversight.\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **AI Usage Compliance Register**, Pre-Deployment Security Clearance Form, Bias Audit Report.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• No audit conclusion or half-margin may be issued based solely on automated LLM output without manual voucher verification.\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Audit Case**: An autonomous body implemented an automated AI loan-waiver system that systematically excluded rural beneficiaries due to biased training dataset.\n• **Audit Finding**: Algorithmic discrimination and violation of programmatic guidelines resulting in denial of benefits.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### Responsible AI, Data Privacy and Public LLM Risks in Audit\n\nnal power, and the availability of vast data. It has evolved from a theore cal concept to a transforma ve technology with far-reaching social acceptance and economic implica ons across diverse domains, in the last decade. Figure 1: Milestones in AI Development As AI con nues to evolve, its poten al to transform industries and drive innova on has assumed immense propor ons. For instance, in the present-day healthcare sector, AI algorithms are analysing medical images for early disease detec on, accelera ng drug discovery etc. The ﬁnancial sector leverages AI for fraud detec on, risk assessment, modelling etc. AI now powers virtual assistants and chatbots, recommenda on systems, and self-driving cars. AI systems have over the years rapidly converged and in some cases, even surpassed the human performance and capabili es in image recogni on, reading comprehension, language understanding etc. (Refer Image below) Figure 2: AI Development h ps://www.isaca.org/resources/glossary Artiﬁcial Intelligence Strategy Framework However, at the same me, like with any emerging technology, the adop on of AI has also raised important social and ethical considera ons, such as data privacy, algorithmic bias, and job displacement, etc. among others. 1.1. Ar ﬁcial Intelligence - Concepts AI technology involves various components, which start with a corpus of a large amount of data, which can be in diﬀerent forms such as text, images, etc. This data is then fed into machine learning models, which are algorithms designed to learn pa erns and rela onships. The popular models are Neural Networks, Decision trees, and Support vector machines etc. They are trained using learning algorithms, such as Supervised learning, Unsupervised learning, or Reinforcement learning (explained in 1.1 below). This enables the models to improve their performance on a given task over me. This requires signiﬁcant compu ng power, usually in the form of powerful processors (like Graphics Processing Units or GPUs), which is required to train and run these models eﬃciently. So ware libraries and frameworks provide pre-built tools and components for building, training, and deploying AI models. Development and deployment tools help to manage the AI model lifecycle, including data prepara on, model training, tes ng, deployment, and monitoring. Finally, a user interface is devised, such as a graphical user interface (GUI), natural language interface, or API (Applica on Programming Interface) which allows humans to interact with and u lize the AI system. Figure 3: AI systems process ﬂow Cogni ve AI and Genera ve AI are two approaches within the broader spectrum of ar ﬁcial intelligence. Cogni ve AI focuses on mimicking human thought processes, emphasizing tasks such as percep on, reasoning, learning, and problem-solving. Genera ve AI is oriented towards crea ng fresh content, o en leveraging new methods like deep learning techniques. It excels in tasks involving crea ve outputs, including genera ng images, music, text, and even en re virtual environments. Both these approaches of Ar ﬁcial Intelligence encompass several key components, each playing a dis nct role in enabling machines to exhibit intelligent behavior. These components may work alone or in tandem to create powerful AI systems capable of learning, perceiving, solving problems, reasoning, and understanding human language. Artiﬁcial Intelligence Strategy Framework",
            "gist": "AI provides deep audit insights but public LLMs pose severe data leak risks. Never expose government data to public AI. Responsible AI demands explainability, fairness, and human oversight.",
            "crux": "1. Never upload official or confidential data to public LLMs.\n2. AI audit findings must be explainable (Explainable AI - XAI).\n3. Human-in-the-loop: Human auditor bears ultimate responsibility.",
