            "title": "2.1 DBMS and RDBMS Basics: Designing databases and principle of normalization",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: CAG IT Audit Manual 2024: Chapter 4 (Database Controls & Integrity)\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: IT Audit Manual Paras 4.1 to 4.25; Codal Standards for Relational Databases\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, verification criteria, and practical audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **RDBMS Architecture**: Tables (relations), Rows (tuples), Columns (attributes); ACID Properties (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability).\n• **Key Integrity Rules**: Entity Integrity (Primary key cannot be NULL), Referential Integrity (Foreign key must match valid primary key or be NULL).\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **Database Schema Register**, Data Dictionary Form DB-01, Database Access Authorization Matrix.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• Direct database modifications (via backend SQL updates) bypassing application business logic are barred; any emergency update requires documented change approval.\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Audit Query Scenario**: Audit executes SQL check: `SELECT child_id FROM child_table WHERE parent_id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM parent_table);`\n• **Audit Finding**: Identification of 450 orphaned voucher payments indicating disabled referential integrity constraints.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### 2.1 DBMS and RDBMS Fundamentals: Database Architecture & Principles of Normalization\n\n#### 1. Concept of Database Management Systems (DBMS)\nA Database Management System (DBMS) is specialized system software that facilitates the creation, maintenance, querying, and administration of structured data repositories.\n- **Limitations of Traditional Flat-File Systems**:\n  - *Data Redundancy and Inconsistency*: Identical employee or pensioner data duplicated across multiple departmental text files.\n  - *Difficulty in Accessing Data*: Inability to run ad-hoc queries across multiple tables without writing custom programs.\n  - *Data Isolation*: Data scattered in diverse proprietary file formats.\n  - *Integrity Constraints*: Inability to enforce cross-table validation rules (e.g., ensuring an account balance never drops below zero).\n  - *Concurrent Access Anomalies*: Simultaneous updates leading to lost updates or uncommitted reads.\n- **Relational Database Management System (RDBMS)**:\n  - Formulated by Dr. E.F. Codd (1970). Organizes data into two-dimensional tables termed **Relations**, consisting of rows (**Tuples** or records) and columns (**Attributes** or fields).\n  - Common Enterprise RDBMS: Oracle Database, Microsoft SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL.\n\n#### 2. Principles of Database Normalization\nNormalization is the systematic mathematical technique of organizing data in a database to: (a) eliminate undesirable data redundancy, and (b) avoid update, insertion, and deletion anomalies.\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        DATABASE NORMALIZATION STAGES (1NF TO BCNF)                |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| FIRST NORMAL FORM (1NF)    | Eliminate repeating groups; ensure all attribute     |\n|                            | values are ATOMIC (indivisible single values).       |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| SECOND NORMAL FORM (2NF)   | Must be in 1NF + Eliminate PARTIAL FUNCTIONAL        |\n|                            | DEPENDENCIES (every non-key attribute fully dependent|\n|                            | on the whole composite primary key).                 |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| THIRD NORMAL FORM (3NF)    | Must be in 2NF + Eliminate TRANSITIVE DEPENDENCIES   |\n|                            | (no non-key attribute dependent on another non-key). |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| BOYCE-CODD FORM (BCNF)     | Strict 3NF: For every functional dependency X -> Y,   |\n|                            | the determinant X MUST BE A SUPER KEY.               |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n- **The Three Normalization Anomalies**:\n  1. *Insertion Anomaly*: Inability to insert a record for one entity without inserting an unrelated entity (e.g., cannot add a new course without first enrolling a student).\n  2. *Deletion Anomaly*: Deleting one piece of data causes unintended loss of other vital information (e.g., deleting the last student enrolled in a department inadvertently deletes the department record).\n  3. *Update (Modification) Anomaly*: Inconsistent data updates resulting from updating a duplicated value in one record while leaving other copies unchanged.",
            "gist": "DBMS three-schema architecture ensures data independence. Normalization removes update anomalies: 1NF (atomic values), 2NF (no partial dependency), 3NF (no transitive dependency).",
            "crux": "1. 1NF mandates atomic attribute values.\n2. 2NF removes partial dependency on composite keys.\n3. 3NF removes transitive dependency ($X \\rightarrow Y \\rightarrow Z$).\n4. Normalization minimizes redundancy and data corruption.",
