            "id": "pc3_s3_c13",
            "chapterNum": 13,
            "title": "3.13 Aadhaar Act and subsequent amendments",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: Aadhaar (Targeted Delivery of Financial and Other Subsidies, Benefits and Services) Act, 2016\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: Sections 1 to 59; Section 7 (Proof of Aadhaar for subsidies), Section 29 (Restriction on sharing identity information), Section 33 (Disclosure), Section 38 (Penalty for unauthorized access)\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, verification criteria, and practical audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Core Biometric Information**: Fingerprints and iris scans; never shareable under any circumstances (Section 29(1)).\n• **Aadhaar Masking**: Only first 8 digits masked (XXXX-XXXX-1234) can be displayed publicly; printing full 12-digit Aadhaar number is illegal.\n• **Section 38 Penalty**: Imprisonment up to **3 years** and fine up to ₹10,000 (₹1 Lakh for companies) for unauthorized database access.\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **Aadhaar Consent Form**, Sub-AUA Agreement, UIDAI Security Compliance Audit Checklist.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• Section 7 mandate: If an individual has not been assigned an Aadhaar number, they must be offered alternate identification and enrollment facilities without denying welfare benefits.\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Audit Observation**: District Welfare Officer published list of 5,000 scholarship recipients displaying complete 12-digit Aadhaar numbers and bank details on notice boards.\n• **Audit Finding**: Criminal violation of Section 29(4) of Aadhaar Act 2016 attracting penal prosecution.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### 3.13 The Aadhaar Act, 2016 & Statutory Amendments: Architecture & Privacy Safeguards\n\n#### 1. Statutory Architecture & Objective\nThe **Aadhaar (Targeted Delivery of Financial and Other Subsidies, Benefits and Services) Act, 2016 (Act 18 of 2016)** provides statutory backing to the world's largest biometric digital identity platform administered by the **Unique Identification Authority of India (UIDAI)**.\n- **Objective**: Ensure targeted, transparent delivery of subsidies, benefits, and services funded out of the Consolidated Fund of India by eliminating ghost, duplicate, and fictitious beneficiaries through 12-digit unique Aadhaar numbers.\n\n#### 2. Landmark Supreme Court Ruling (2018) & Key Safeguards\nIn *Justice K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2018)*, a five-judge Constitution Bench upheld the constitutional validity of Aadhaar for welfare subsidies while striking down unconstitutional provisions:\n- **Upheld Section 7**: Mandatory requirement of Aadhaar for receiving state subsidies, benefits, or services funded from the Consolidated Fund of India (food rations, LPG subsidy, PM-KISAN, MGNREGA).\n- **Struck Down Section 57 (Commercial Use)**: Declared unconstitutional the use of Aadhaar by private commercial corporations (telecom firms, private banks, fintechs) for mandatory verification.\n- **Bank Accounts & Mobile SIMs**: Ruled that mandatory linking of Aadhaar with bank accounts and mobile phone SIM cards is unconstitutional and invalid.\n- **School Admissions**: Ruled that Aadhaar cannot be made compulsory for school admissions or competitive examinations (NEET, JEE).\n\n#### 3. Critical Statutory Provisions of the Aadhaar Act\n1. **Section 29 (Strict Prohibition on Sharing Core Biometric Information)**:\n   - Core biometric information (fingerprints and iris scans) collected under the Act can **never be shared with anyone for any reason whatsoever**, or used for any purpose other than generation of Aadhaar and authentication.\n2. **Section 33 (Disclosure of Information)**:\n   - Identity information and authentication records can be disclosed **only** pursuant to an order of a court not inferior to that of a **High Court Judge** (amended; previously District Judge).\n   - In matters of national security, disclosure may be authorized by an officer not below the rank of **Secretary to the Government of India**, reviewed by an Oversight Committee chaired by the Cabinet Secretary.\n3. **Virtual ID (VID) & Aadhaar Masking**:\n   - Citizens can generate a temporary 16-digit revocable Virtual ID (VID) to authenticate without disclosing their actual 12-digit Aadhaar number.\n   - *Masked Aadhaar*: Displays only the last 4 digits (e.g., `xxxx-xxxx-1234`), concealing the first 8 digits to protect privacy.",
            "gist": "Aadhaar Act 2016 authorizes Section 7 authentication for Consolidated Fund subsidies. Section 29 strictly prohibits sharing or publishing core biometrics and mandates masking.",
            "crux": "1. Sec 7: Aadhaar mandatory for Consolidated Fund benefits/subsidies.\n2. Sec 29: Publishing core biometrics is strictly prohibited by law.\n3. Audit verifies DBT transactions while ensuring PII masking.",
