            "id": "pc4_s1_c6",
            "chapterNum": 6,
            "title": "2. Fundamental Rules & Supplementary Rules (Part II): Travelling Allowance Rules",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: Supplementary Rules (SR Part II - Travelling Allowance Rules)\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: SR 17 to SR 195; Ministry of Finance OM on 7th CPC TA/DA Entitlements\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, verification criteria, and practical audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Pay Level Slabs for TA/DA**:\n  - Level 14 & above: Air Business Class, Hotel up to ₹7,500/day, Food ₹1,200/day.\n  - Level 9 to 13: Air Economy Class, Hotel up to ₹4,500/day, Food ₹1,000/day.\n  - Level 6 to 8: AC II Tier / Economy Air, Hotel up to ₹2,250/day, Food ₹900/day.\n• **Daily Allowance (DA) Slabs**: Absence > 12 hours: 100%; Absence 6 to 12 hours: 70%; Absence < 6 hours: 30%.\n• **TA Claim Time Ceiling**: Must be submitted within **strictly 60 days** of completion of journey; claim forfeits if delayed beyond 60 days unless condoned.\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **TA Bill Form (GAR 14A)**, Tour Diary / Tour Program Sanction, Hotel/Food Receipts Register.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• Air travel tickets must be booked exclusively through authorized travel agents (Balmer Lawrie, Ashoka Travels, IRCTC); tickets booked through unauthorized private agents are strictly barred from reimbursement.\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **SAS Problem**: Level 7 official travelled by flight without authorization and submitted TA claim after 90 days with tickets booked via private travel website.\n• **Audit Disallowance**: 1. Unentitled travel mode (restricted to AC II train fare); 2. Forfeiture under 60-day rule; 3. Breach of authorized agent mandate.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### 2. Fundamental Rules & Supplementary Rules (Part II): Travelling Allowance Rules\n\nThe **Travelling Allowance (TA) Rules**, codified under the **Supplementary Rules (SR 17 to SR 195)**, govern the compensatory reimbursement of expenses incurred by central government employees while traveling in the interest of public service. Travelling allowance is designed to ensure that an officer is not out of pocket when performing official tours or transfers, but it is explicitly mandated that **TA shall not be a source of profit** to the recipient.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Classification of Officers and Travel Entitlements (7th CPC)\nUnder the **7th Central Pay Commission (CPC)** recommendations notified by the Ministry of Finance, government servants are categorized into travel entitlement grades based exclusively on their **Level in the Pay Matrix**:\n\n```\n                       7th CPC TRAVEL ENTITLEMENT TIERS\n                                       |\n        +------------------------------+------------------------------+\n        |                                                             |\nPAY MATRIX LEVEL 14 & ABOVE                                   PAY MATRIX LEVEL 9 TO 13A\n- Air Travel: Business / Club Class                            - Air Travel: Economy Class\n- Rail Travel: AC First Class / Executive Class                - Rail Travel: AC First Class / Executive Class\n- Premium Trains: Executive Class                             - Premium Trains: AC First / Chair Car\n- Road: AC Taxi reimbursement                                  - Road: AC Taxi reimbursement\n\nPAY MATRIX LEVEL 6 TO 8                                       PAY MATRIX LEVEL 5 & BELOW\n- Air Travel: Not entitled (Rail only)                         - Air Travel: Not entitled\n- Rail Travel: AC II Tier / AC Chair Car                       - Rail Travel: AC III Tier / Sleeper Class\n- Road: Non-AC Taxi / Autorickshaw                             - Road: Autorickshaw / Bus\n```\n\n- **Authorized Travel Agents**: Tickets for air travel must be booked exclusively through three authorized travel agents: **Balmer Lawrie & Co.**, **Ashok Travels & Tours (ATT)**, and **Indian Railway Catering and Tourism Corporation (IRCTC)**, choosing the lowest available fare within the scheduled time slot.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Components of Daily Allowance (DA) on Tour\nUnder 7th CPC rules, the traditional fixed DA per-diem rates were replaced by a reimbursement-based system across three distinct components:\n1. **Hotel Accommodation Charges**:\n   - Reimbursed against production of valid tax invoices up to prescribed ceilings based on Pay Level:\n     - Level 14 and above: Up to **Rs 7,500 per day**.\n     - Level 12 and 13: Up to **Rs 4,500 per day**.\n     - Level 9 to 11: Up to **Rs 2,250 per day**.\n     - Level 6 to 8: Up to **Rs 750 per day**.\n2. **Reimbursement of Food Charges**:\n   - Lump-sum daily reimbursement without requiring food bills:\n     - Level 14 and above: **Rs 1,200 per day**.\n     - Level 12 and 13: **Rs 1,000 per day**.\n     - Level 9 to 11: **Rs 900 per day**.\n     - Level 6 to 8: **Rs 800 per day**.\n     - Level 5 and below: **Rs 500 per day**.\n3. **Reimbursement of Local Conveyance**:\n   - Reimbursed based on actual travel vouchers (taxi/auto/public transport) up to prescribed mileage rates.\n4. **Graduation of DA based on Absence from Headquarters**:\n   - Absence $< 6$ hours: **30 percent** of lump-sum food component.\n   - Absence $6$ to $12$ hours: **70 percent** of lump-sum food component.\n   - Absence $> 12$ hours: **100 percent** of lump-sum food component.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Travelling Allowance on Transfer\nWhen an employee is transferred in the public interest involving a change of station, entitlements comprise:\n1. **Composite Transfer Grant (CTG)**:\n   - A lump-sum grant equal to **80 percent of the last month's Basic Pay** (enhanced to 100% of Basic Pay for transfers to or from the island territories of Andaman & Nicobar and Lakshadweep).\n   - In case of transfer within the same station or $< 20$ km involving a mandatory change of official residence, CTG is paid at **one-third (33.3%)** of the admissible rate.\n2. **Fares for Self and Family**:\n   - Actual fares for the officer and members of his family (as defined under SR 2(8)) according to his entitled class.\n3. **Transportation of Personal Effects**:\n   - Levels 12 and above: Up to **6,000 kg by goods train / one 40-foot container**.\n   - Levels 6 to 11: Up to **6,000 kg by goods train / one 20-foot container**.\n   - Lower Levels: 3,000 kg to 1,500 kg.\n   - Road transport rates reimbursed at standardized per-km rates notified by the local Director of Transport.\n4. **Transportation of Personal Conveyance**:\n   - Reimbursement of freight charges for transporting one motor car or one motorcycle/scooter by rail or road based on entitlement.\n\n---\n\n### 4. TA on Retirement and Submission Timelines\n- **Retirement TA**: A retiring government servant is entitled to transfer TA (CTG at 80%, passenger fares, and luggage transportation) to enable him and his family to settle down at his declared Home Town or any other chosen place of settlement within India.\n- **Limitation Period for TA Claims**:\n  - Tour TA claims must be submitted within **sixty days** following the completion of the journey.\n  - Transfer TA claims must be submitted within **sixty days** of the journey.\n  - Failure to prefer claims within the limitation period results in forfeiture of the claim unless specifically condoned by the competent authority.\n",
            "gist": "TA entitlements depend on Pay Matrix Levels. Composite Transfer Grant (CTG) is 80% of basic pay on inter-station transfer. LTC operates on a 4-year block.",
            "crux": "1. CTG = 80% of Basic Pay for transfers beyond 20 km.\n2. LTC 4-year block: Home Town twice, or 1 Home Town + 1 Anywhere in India.\n3. Fresh recruits get 8 years special LTC benefits.",
