            "title": "1. Government Accounting Rules, 1990 - Chapter on General outline of the system of Accounts",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: Government Accounting Rules, 1990 (GAR 1990)\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: Rules 1 to 40: Rule 10 (Period of accounts), Rule 13 (Currency of accounts), Rule 25 (Single vs Double Entry), Rule 26 (Three parts of government accounts), Rule 30 (Classification criteria)\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, verification criteria, and practical audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Three Parts of Government Accounts (Rule 26)**:\n  - Part I: Consolidated Fund of India/State (Revenue, Capital, Debt & Loans).\n  - Part II: Contingency Fund of India/State (Advances for unforeseen expenditure).\n  - Part III: Public Account (Debt other than CFI, Deposits, Advances, Remittances, Suspense).\n• **Financial Year (Rule 10)**: 1st April to 31st March.\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **Monthly Account Form GAR-1**, Annual Appropriation Accounts, Finance Accounts Formats.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• Expenditure of a capital nature must not be met from Revenue head, and operational maintenance expenditure must not be capitalized under Capital heads (Rule 30).\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Codal Classification Problem**: Government incurred expenditure of ₹5.00 Crore on painting, whitewashing, and routine repair of district office buildings and booked it under Major Head 4059 (Capital Outlay).\n• **Audit Correction**: Violation of GAR 1990 Rule 30; must be reclassified under Major Head 2059 (Revenue Expenditure).\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### 1. Government Accounting Rules, 1990 - Chapter on General outline of the system of Accounts\n\nThe **Government Accounting Rules, 1990 [GAR 1990]** were framed by the President on the advice of the **Comptroller and Auditor General of India (CAG)** under **Article 150** of the Constitution. Chapter 2 of GAR 1990 (*General Outline of the System of Accounts*, Rules 18 to 39) establishes the fundamental architecture of public accounts in India, governing the three-part fund division, cash accounting basis, and inter-governmental reconciliation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three-Part Division of Government Accounts (Rule 18)\nGovernment accounts are maintained in three distinct constitutional parts:\n\n```\n                         THE THREE-PART DIVISION OF ACCOUNTS\n                                          |\n         +--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\n         |                                |                                |\n   PART I: CONSOLIDATED FUND        PART II: CONTINGENCY FUND        PART III: PUBLIC ACCOUNT\n   - Divided into Revenue,          - Imprest fund for urgent,       - All other public moneys\n     Capital & Debt Sections          unforeseen expenditure           received by Government\n   - Revenue: Tax, Non-tax,         - Recouped via Supplementary     - GPF, Deposits, Advances,\n     Grants, & revenue spends         Grants under Art 115 / 205       Suspense & Remittances\n   - Capital: Infrastructure        - Authorized by executive;       - Government acts as banker /\n     assets & loan recoveries         recoupment voted by House        trustee; no voting needed\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. General Principles Governing Classification and Accounting\n1. **Cash Basis of Accounting (Rule 23)**:\n   - With minor exceptions for commercial undertakings, government accounts are maintained on a **strict cash basis**:\n   - Transactions are recorded in the accounts only when actual cash or cheques are received or disbursed. Outstanding liabilities, accrued income, or unpaid commitments are **not recognized** in the annual accounts.\n2. **Form and Division of Functional Accounts**:\n   - **Part I - Consolidated Fund**:\n     - *Revenue Account*: Deals with the proceeds of taxation, duties, and ordinary non-tax revenues, along with expenditure met from them.\n     - *Capital Account*: Records expenditure incurred with the object of either increasing concrete assets of a material and permanent character, or reducing recurring liabilities.\n     - *Debt & Loan Section*: Records public debt raised and repaid, along with loans and advances issued by the Government and recoveries thereof.\n   - **Part III - Public Account**:\n     - *Debt (other than Consolidated Fund)*: Small savings, provident funds.\n     - *Deposits and Advances*: Moneys deposited by civil courts, contractors (security deposits), and departmental advances.\n     - *Suspense and Remittances*: Transitory clearing heads for transactions awaiting final adjustment or transfer between accounting circles.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Inter-Governmental Adjustments and RBI Settlement (Rules 33 to 39)\n- **Central Accounts Section (CAS), RBI Nagpur**:\n  - Functions as the national clearinghouse for all inter-governmental financial settlements between the Union Government and State Governments, and between different State Governments.\n  - Balances are adjusted daily by debiting or crediting the respective government's cash balance maintained with the RBI.\n- **Adjustment of Transactions**:\n  - No physical money moves between governments; adjustments are carried out through **Inter-Governmental Advices (IGAs)** and **Clearance Memos** transmitted electronically to CAS, RBI Nagpur.\n",
            "gist": "GAR 1990 outlines the 6-tier classification code: Major Head (Function), Sub-Major, Minor Head (Program), Sub-Head (Scheme), Detailed Head, and Object Head (Economic Nature). Accounts are on cash basis.",
            "crux": "1. 6-tier accounting classification code.\n2. Major Head = 4 digits; Minor Head = 3 digits; Object Head = 2 digits.\n3. Cash basis: Transactions recorded when cash/cheque is actually paid/received.",
