            "chapterNum": 1,
            "title": "(i) Time schedule and Instructions for preparation and submission of Accounts",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: MSO (A&E) Volume-I: Chapter 19 & Manual of State Finance Accounts\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: MSO Paras 19.1 to 19.35; Annual Time Schedule issued by CAG HQ for Finance and Appropriation Accounts\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, accounting verification, and audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Strict Compilation Milestones**:\n  - 31st May: Completion of March (Final) Accounts.\n  - 30th June: Completion of March (Supplementary) Accounts.\n  - 31st July: Submission of Draft Finance and Appropriation Accounts to AG (Audit).\n  - 30th September: Submission of finalized signed Bond Copies to CAG of India.\n  - 31st December: Placement of printed accounts before State Legislature.\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **Time Schedule Matrix**, Letter of Transmittal, Certificate of Transmission.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• Supplementary accounts cannot be kept open beyond 30th June without formal prior sanction from Headquarters (CAG).\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Administrative Delay Case**: State AG office delayed submission of draft accounts by 4 months due to non-receipt of treasury accounts from 12 treasuries.\n• **Codal Mandate**: Compile accounts with available records, booking missing treasury figures under 'Treasury Suspense' under MSO Para 19.8.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### Time Schedule & Instructions for Preparation and Submission of Finance & Appropriation Accounts\n\n#### 1. Annual Calendar of State Government Accounts\nUnder Section 10 and 11 of the Comptroller and Auditor General's (Duties, Powers and Conditions of Service) Act, 1971, the Accountant General (A&E) is charged with compiling the **Annual Finance Accounts** and **Appropriation Accounts** of the State Government.\n- **The Annual Accounting Cycle**:\n  - *April to March*: Receipt of monthly accounts from Treasuries and Public Works/Forest Divisions; compilation of Monthly Civil Accounts.\n  - *April - May*: Closing of March (Preliminary) accounts.\n  - *June*: Processing of **March (Supplementary) Accounts**. Captures inter-governmental book adjustments, transfer entries, and clearing of suspense balances.\n  - *15th July*: Compilation of the **Draft Finance Accounts** and **Draft Appropriation Accounts** by AG (A&E).\n  - *1st August*: Formal transmission of Draft Accounts to the **Accountant General (Audit)** for statutory audit check.\n  - *August - September*: Audit scrutiny by AG (Audit), settlement of audit observations, joint reconciliation, and signing of certificates.\n  - *15th October*: Printing of **Bond Copies** (hardbound official copies) and submission to the CAG Headquarters in New Delhi for the C&AG's countersignature.\n  - *Winter Session of State Legislature*: Submission by CAG to the Governor under Article 151(2) for laying before the State Legislative Assembly.\n\n#### 2. Key Instructions and Safeguards in Account Preparation\n1. **Reconciliation of March Supplementary Entries**:\n   - The AG (A&E) ensures that no cash transactions are booked in March Supplementary accounts; only book adjustments, rectifications, and inter-governmental clearances are permitted.\n2. **Elimination of Minus Balances**:\n   - A minus balance under a service expenditure head indicates serious accounting distortion (e.g., recoveries exceeding expenditure or incorrect debit booking). All minus balances must be investigated and resolved before accounts closure.\n3. **Reconciliation with Treasury & RBI**:\n   - The closing cash balance of the State Government must be reconciled to the last paisa with the certified statement of closing balance issued by the **Central Accounts Section (CAS), Reserve Bank of India, Nagpur**.",
