            "chapterNum": 2,
            "title": "(ii) Form of Appropriation Accounts & Statement of recoveries adjusted in reduction of expenditure",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: Government Accounting Rules 1990 & Manual of Standing Orders (A&E) Vol-I\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: GAR Rule 26; MSO (A&E) Chapter 20; Form of Appropriation Accounts\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, accounting verification, and audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Appropriation Accounts Architecture**: Grant-wise presentation showing Original Grant, Supplementary Grant, Total Grant, Actual Expenditure, Saving (-) or Excess (+).\n• **Significant Variations**: Explanations required for variations exceeding prescribed thresholds (e.g., Saving/Excess $> ₹50$ Lakhs and $> 10\\%$ of grant).\n• **Statement of Recoveries**: Recoveries adjusted in reduction of expenditure tabulated separately.\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **Grant Summary Form**, Detailed Appropriation Account by Sub-Head, Statement of Recoveries Form.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• Expenditure incurred in excess of voted grant cannot be regularized by savings under other grants (violates Article 204/205 of Constitution).\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Practical Audit Scenario**: Grant No. 12 (Public Works) showed an unapproved excess expenditure of ₹14.80 Crore under Major Head 3054.\n• **Codal Action**: Must be reported in Appropriation Accounts as an unvoted excess requiring legislative regularization under Article 205.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### Form of Appropriation Accounts & Statement of Recoveries Adjusted in Reduction of Expenditure\n\n#### 1. Statutory Architecture of Appropriation Accounts\nThe Appropriation Accounts present an audited comparison between the expenditure authorized by the Legislature under the Appropriation Act and the actual expenditure incurred during the financial year.\n- **Grant-Wise Tabular Structure**:\n  ```\n  GRANT NO. 14 - HIGHER EDUCATION\n  (All figures in Thousands of Rupees)\n  \n  Section / Head           Original Grant    Supplementary    Total Grant    Actual Exp.    Saving (-) / Excess (+)\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  REVENUE:\n    Voted                    12,50,00,00         45,00,00     12,95,00,00    11,80,25,40           (-) 1,14,74,60\n    Charged                        50,00            10,00           60,00          48,20               (-) 11,80\n  CAPITAL:\n    Voted                     3,20,00,00         80,00,00      4,00,00,00     4,15,60,80           (+) 15,60,80\n    Charged                         ...              ...             ...            ...                     ...\n  ```\n\n#### 2. Standards for Commenting on Variations\nUnder C&AG guidelines, every sub-head variation between Total Grant and Actual Expenditure exceeding prescribed quantitative thresholds must be supported by explanatory notes:\n- **Monetary Thresholds for Comments**:\n  - *Savings*: Where savings under a sub-head exceed **₹50 Lakh** (or ₹1 Crore in major States) and constitute more than **10% to 20%** of the provision.\n  - *Excesses*: Any excess expenditure over the grant, even of one rupee, must be explained because it represents unconstitutional expenditure under Article 204.\n- **Standard Classification of Reasons**:\n  - Genuine economy vs. non-implementation of schemes.\n  - Late receipt of central assistance.\n  - Non-receipt of administrative sanctions or claims from field offices.\n\n#### 3. Statement of Recoveries Adjusted in Reduction of Expenditure (Gross vs. Net Budgeting)\n- **Constitutional Principle**: In India, the demands presented to the Legislature are for **Gross Expenditure** (the total money required without taking into account anticipated recoveries).\n- **The Accounting Statement**:\n  - The Appropriation Accounts include a mandatory Appendix: *Statement of Recoveries Adjusted in Reduction of Expenditure*.\n  - Explains the difference between the **Gross Expenditure** shown in the Appropriation Accounts and the **Net Expenditure** shown in the Finance Accounts.\n  - *Examples of Deduct Recoveries*: Recovery of overpayments of prior years, transfer from reserve funds, salvage receipts from capital works credited as reduction of capital expenditure under Rule 26 of GAR 1990.",
            "gist": "Appropriation Accounts present grant-wise expenditure vs budget (Original + Supplementary). Variations beyond thresholds require detailed administrative explanations.",
            "crux": "1. Voted and Charged expenditures are kept strictly separate.\n2. Excess expenditure over grant is an unconstitutional irregularity under Art 205.\n3. Recoveries in reduction of expenditure are reconciled separately.",
            "flashcards": [
              {
                "term": "(ii) Form of Appropriation Accounts & St",
                "def": "Appropriation Accounts present grant-wise expenditure vs budget (Original + Supplementary). Variations beyond thresholds"
              }
            ],
