            "id": "pc11_s5_c1",
            "chapterNum": 1,
            "title": "(i) Constitution of Government Accounting Standards Advisory Board (GASAB), structure and responsibilities",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: Government Accounting Standards Advisory Board (GASAB) Charter\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: Ministry of Finance Notification (2002); Constitution of GASAB under Article 150\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, accounting verification, and audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Composition of GASAB**: Chaired by Deputy CAG; members include Secretary (Expenditure), Controller General of Accounts (CGA), Controller General of Defence Accounts (CGDA), Financial Commissioner (Railways), Member (Finance) Telecom, 4 State Finance Secretaries, RBI representative, ICAI President.\n• **Mandate**: Formulate and recommend government accounting standards for Union and States to improve financial reporting.\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **Exposure Draft Format**, Consultation Paper, Standard Notification Memo.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• Standards formulated by GASAB take statutory effect only after formal notification by Ministry of Finance on advice of CAG under Article 150.\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Exam Question**: Describe the constitution and key responsibilities of GASAB.\n• **Key Points**: Established in 2002, chaired by Dy. CAG, multistakeholder representation; formulates IGAS (cash-based) and IGFRS (accrual-based) standards.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### Constitution of Government Accounting Standards Advisory Board (GASAB)\n\n#### 1. Genesis, Constitution & Institutional Purpose\nThe **Government Accounting Standards Advisory Board (GASAB)** was established by the Comptroller and Auditor General of India in August 2002 with the formal support of the Ministry of Finance.\n- **Primary Mandate**:\n  - Formulate and recommend high-quality **Indian Government Accounting Standards (IGAS)** for cash-based accounting.\n  - Formulate **Indian Government Financial Reporting Standards (IGFRS)** for accrual-based accounting.\n  - Formulate frameworks for Natural Resource Accounting (NRA).\n  - Bridge the gap between disparate accounting practices across States and enhance transparency in public accounts.\n\n#### 2. High-Level Composition of the Board\nGASAB is a multi-stakeholder representative body comprising:\n- **Chairperson**: Deputy Comptroller and Auditor General (Government Accounts).\n- **Members**:\n  - Controller General of Accounts (CGA).\n  - Controller General of Defence Accounts (CGDA).\n  - Financial Commissioner, Railway Board.\n  - Member (Finance), Department of Telecommunications.\n  - Additional Secretary (Budget), Ministry of Finance.\n  - Principal Secretaries (Finance) of four representative States (selected on a rotational basis).\n  - Representative of the Reserve Bank of India.\n  - President / Representative of the Institute of Chartered Accountants of India (ICAI).",
            "gist": "GASAB was established in 2002 to formulate standards for government accounts (IGAS for cash accounting and IGFRS for accrual transition).",
            "crux": "1. Constituted by CAG with Ministry of Finance backing.\n2. Chaired by Deputy CAG.\n3. Formulates IGAS (cash) and IGFRS (accrual) standards.",
            "flashcards": [
              {
                "term": "(i) Constitution of Government Accountin",
                "def": "GASAB was established in 2002 to formulate standards for government accounts (IGAS for cash accounting and IGFRS for acc"
              }
            ],
            "sourceDoc": "Civil Accounts Manual, GAR 1990, LMMH & GASAB Compendiums"
