            "id": "pc13_s1_c1",
            "chapterNum": 1,
            "title": "a. Government budget – meaning, objectives and components",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: General Financial Rules 2017 (Chapter 3: Budget Formulation and Implementation)\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: GFR 2017 Rules 42 to 64; Constitution of India Article 112; Budget Manual Paras 1 to 25\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, accounting verification, and audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Budget Objectives**: Optimal allocation of resources, macroeconomic stabilization, equitable distribution.\n• **Budget Components**: Revenue Budget (Revenue Receipts and Revenue Expenditure) vs. Capital Budget (Capital Receipts and Capital Outlay / Debt Disbursements).\n• **Financial Year**: 1st April to 31st March (Rule 42).\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **Annual Financial Statement (AFS)**, Demand for Grants Form GFR 1, Expenditure Profile, Statement of Voted and Charged Expenditure.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• No expenditure can be incurred from Consolidated Fund of India without prior legislative appropriation (Article 114(3)).\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Budget Analysis**: Department incurred revenue expenditure of ₹35 Crore out of capital grants without legislative approval.\n• **Audit Finding**: Misclassification of budget expenditure violating Article 112 and GFR Rule 48.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### Government Budget: Meaning, Objectives, Components & Public Account Architecture\n\n#### 1. Definition and Economic Philosophy of Government Budget\nA Government Budget is an authoritative legal, financial, and political document presented annually by the executive to the legislature. It embodies the government's estimated revenues and proposed expenditures for the ensuing fiscal year, reflecting its socio-economic policies, strategic priorities, and fiscal management targets.\n- Unlike a commercial budget, which is primarily an internal management tool for profit planning, a public budget possesses constitutional sanctity. In India, under **Article 265**, *\"No tax shall be levied or collected except by authority of law,\"* and under **Article 266(3)**, *\"No moneys out of the Consolidated Fund of India or the Consolidated Fund of a State shall be appropriated except in accordance with law.\"*\n\n#### 2. Fundamental Objectives of Public Budgeting\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        OBJECTIVES OF PUBLIC BUDGETARY POLICY                      |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 1. REALLOCATION OF RESOURCES    | 2. REDISTRIBUTION OF INCOME & WEALTH            |\n| - Channeling public capital     | - Progressive taxation (direct taxes)           |\n|   into social infrastructure    | - Targeted subsidies, DBT, welfare schemes      |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 3. ECONOMIC STABILIZATION       | 4. MANAGEMENT OF PUBLIC ENTERPRISES             |\n| - Counter-cyclical fiscal policy| - Capital infusion into CPSEs and financial     |\n| - Controlling inflation/slump   |   institutions to drive economic expansion      |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n#### 3. The Three Constitutional Funds of Government (Articles 266 & 267)\nThe entire financial architecture of the Union and State Governments in India operates through three distinct constitutional funds:\n1. **Consolidated Fund of India / State (Article 266(1))**:\n   - The primary reservoir of government finances. All revenues received by Government (tax and non-tax), all loans raised through market borrowings, Treasury Bills, and ways and means advances, and all moneys received in repayment of loans flow into this Fund.\n   - *Parliamentary Control*: Not a single rupee can be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund without an **Appropriation Act** passed by Parliament / State Legislature under Article 114 / 204.\n2. **Contingency Fund of India / State (Article 267)**:\n   - An imprest placed at the disposal of the President of India (or Governor of a State) to enable advances to be made for meeting **unforeseen expenditure** pending authorization by Parliament through supplementary grants.\n   - *Statutory Corpus*: Originally ₹50 Crore, enhanced to ₹500 Crore in 2005, and enhanced by the Finance Act 2021 to a permanent corpus of **₹30,000 Crore**.\n   - Operated by the Secretary, Department of Economic Affairs, Ministry of Finance on behalf of the President. All advances drawn must be recouped to the Fund through supplementary demands.\n3. **Public Account of India / State (Article 266(2))**:\n   - Comprises all other public moneys received by or on behalf of the Government where the Government acts merely as a **banker, trustee, or custodian**.\n   - *Contents*: Small savings, Provident Funds (GPF), Postal Savings, Reserve Funds, Court Deposits, and Remittances.\n   - *Legislative Control*: Moneys in the Public Account do not belong to the Government and are repayable to depositors; hence, withdrawals do **not** require an Appropriation Act.\n\n#### 4. Classification of Budget: Revenue Budget vs. Capital Budget\nThe Government Budget is bifurcated into the **Revenue Budget** and the **Capital Budget**:\n| Budget Component | Inflows / Receipts | Outflows / Expenditures | Economic & Accounting Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Revenue Budget** | **Revenue Receipts**: <br>1. *Tax Revenue*: Direct Taxes (Income Tax, Corporation Tax) and Indirect Taxes (GST, Customs, Excise). <br>2. *Non-Tax Revenue*: Interest on loans to States, Dividends/profits from CPSEs/RBI, user charges, spectrum fees. | **Revenue Expenditure**: Expenses incurred for day-to-day government administration, salaries, pensions, subsidies, defense maintenance, grants to States (under Art 275/282), and interest payments on public debt. | **Does NOT alter asset/liability balance.** Receipts do not create liabilities or reduce assets; expenditures do not create physical/financial assets or reduce liabilities. |\n| **Capital Budget** | **Capital Receipts**: <br>1. *Debt Receipts*: Market loans, external loans, Treasury Bills, NSSF borrowings. <br>2. *Non-Debt Capital Receipts (NDCR)*: Recovery of loans and advances from States/CPSEs, proceeds from disinvestment of public assets. | **Capital Expenditure (CapEx)**: Outlays on acquisition of long-term assets: land, buildings, machinery, highway construction, railways, defense equipment, equity infusion, and loans advanced to State Governments and foreign nations. | **Directly creates assets or reduces liabilities.** Capital receipts create debt obligations or liquidate assets; capital expenditures build national physical assets or liquidate sovereign debt. |",
            "gist": "A government budget balances Revenue Budget (taxes, operational expenses) and Capital Budget (borrowings, physical assets) to achieve allocation, distribution, and stabilization.",
            "crux": "1. Revenue expenditure does not create physical assets.\n2. Capital expenditure results in creation of permanent physical/financial assets.\n3. Budget period is 1st April to 31st March.",
