            "id": "pc13_s1_c5",
            "chapterNum": 5,
            "title": "e. Budget Review (Pre-Budget, Post-Budget, Classification, Supplementary, Announcements & Speech)",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: GFR 2017 (Chapter 3) & Delegation of Financial Powers Rules 1978\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: GFR Rules 53 to 64; DFPR Rule 10; Article 115/116 of Constitution\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, accounting verification, and audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Pre-Budget Review**: Scrutiny of expenditure trends during first 6 months of financial year.\n• **Supplementary Demands**: Presented during Monsoon/Winter/Budget sessions for additional funds.\n• **Re-appropriation Limitations**: No re-appropriation between Voted and Charged, or between Capital and Revenue, or towards New Service.\n• **Rush of Expenditure**: Expenditure in last quarter restricted to **25%** and in March to **10%** of total budget (Rule 62(3)).\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **Supplementary Grant Schedule**, Re-appropriation Order Form, March Expenditure Monitoring Return.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• Savings under one Grant cannot be utilized to meet excesses under another Grant without parliamentary Supplementary Appropriation.\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Rush of Expenditure Case**: Ministry spent 48% of its annual budget grant in the month of March, including ₹80 Crore parked in commercial bank accounts.\n• **Audit Objection**: Severe breach of GFR Rule 62(3) and artificial rush of expenditure.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### Budget Review: Pre-Budget, Post-Budget, Classification, Supplementary Grants & Legislative Scrutiny\n\n#### 1. The Post-Budget Execution & Review Cycle\nOnce the Appropriation Act and Finance Act receive Presidential assent, the execution and continuous monitoring of the budget begins. The budget review process ensures that:\n- Appropriations are utilized strictly for intended purposes without unauthorized diversions.\n- Expenditures do not exceed legislative authorizations.\n- Savings are surrendered in a timely manner.\n- Parliamentary scrutiny is maintained through Standing Committees and the Comptroller and Auditor General.\n\n#### 2. Re-Appropriation Rules & Constraints (GFR Rule 10 & DFPR)\nRe-appropriation means the transfer of funds from one primary unit of appropriation (Sub-head / Detailed head) to another within the same grant.\n- **Strict Statutory Restrictions on Re-Appropriation**:\n  1. *Across Grants Prohibited*: No re-appropriation is permissible from one Demand for Grant to another Demand for Grant. Transfers between different grants require a Supplementary Demand.\n  2. *Voted to Charged Prohibited*: No re-appropriation can be made from a Voted expenditure head to meet a Charged expenditure head, or vice versa.\n  3. *Capital to Revenue Prohibited*: Funds allocated for Capital Expenditure **cannot** be re-appropriated to meet Revenue Expenditure without Ministry of Finance approval.\n  4. *No Re-Appropriation to New Service*: No funds can be re-appropriated to an item of \"New Service\" or \"New Instrument of Service\" without parliamentary approval.\n  5. *Secretariat Approval*: Re-appropriation from scheme allocations to establishment expenses is prohibited without specific concurrence of the Ministry of Finance.\n\n#### 3. Surrender of Savings & Rush of Expenditure (GFR Rules 62 & 64)\n- **Surrender of Savings (GFR Rule 62)**:\n  - Line Ministries and DDOs must monitor progressive expenditure monthly.\n  - Anticipated savings within a grant must be formally surrendered to the Ministry of Finance immediately when identified, and **not later than 15th March** of the financial year.\n  - *Audit Concern*: Holding on to unspent savings until 31st March prevents funds from being re-allocated to priority sectors, leading to artificial lapses.\n- **Rush of Expenditure in March (GFR Rule 64)**:\n  - Rush of expenditure, particularly in the closing month of March, is universally condemned by the Public Accounts Committee as a financial irregularity.\n  - *Mandatory Quarterly Expenditure Management Ceilings*:\n    - Expenditure in the **last quarter (Jan - Mar)** is capped at **33% of the budget allotment**.\n    - Expenditure in the **last month (March)** is capped strictly at **15% of the budget allotment**.\n    - Parking of funds in commercial bank accounts or civil deposits at year-end to evade budget lapse is an actionable fiscal irregularity.\n\n#### 4. Parliamentary Financial Committees\nParliament exercises post-budget legislative scrutiny through three specialized financial committees assisted by the C&AG:\n1. **Public Accounts Committee (PAC)**:\n   - Comprises 22 members (15 Lok Sabha, 7 Rajya Sabha). By convention, chaired by a member of the Opposition.\n   - Examines the **Appropriation Accounts** and **Finance Accounts** of the Union Government along with the official **Audit Reports of the C&AG**.\n   - Ascertains whether money spent was legally available, whether expenditure conforms to the authority governing it, and examines cases of excess expenditure over grants.\n2. **Estimates Committee**:\n   - Comprises 30 members exclusively from the Lok Sabha.\n   - Examines budget estimates to suggest economies, organizational improvements, and administrative efficiency.\n3. **Committee on Public Undertakings (CoPU)**:\n   - Comprises 22 members (15 Lok Sabha, 7 Rajya Sabha).\n   - Examines the reports and accounts of Central Public Sector Enterprises (CPSEs) and CAG Commercial Audit Reports.",
            "gist": "Joint Budget Review by AG Audit & A&E verifies classification correctness, persistent savings/excesses, genuineness of supplementary grants, and Budget Speech promises.",
            "crux": "1. Jointly conducted by AG (Audit) and AG (A&E).\n2. Governed by CAG Headquarters Checklist dated 6 April 2022.\n3. Checks classification compliance with LMMH and detects unfunded speech promises.",
