            "id": "pc13_s3_c3",
            "chapterNum": 3,
            "title": "c. Deficit management through internal & external debt",
            "content": "### Tier 1: Syllabus Anchor & Statutory Codal Authority\n- **Prescribed Authority**: Public Debt Management Manual (RBI & Ministry of Finance)\n- **Relevant Codal Provisions**: Public Debt Act 1944 / Government Securities Act 2006; RBI Debt Guidelines\n- **CAG Examination Relevance**: Tests statutory compliance, accounting verification, and audit drafting.\n\n### Tier 2: Statutory Provisions, Definitions & Prescribed Thresholds\n• **Internal Debt**: Market Loans (Dated G-Secs), Treasury Bills (91-day, 182-day, 364-day), Special Securities, Sovereign Gold Bonds.\n• **External Debt**: Multilateral (World Bank, ADB) and Bilateral loans; denominated in foreign currencies.\n• **Weighted Average Cost (WAC)**: Average coupon yield on outstanding debt portfolio.\n• **Rollover Risk**: Proportion of debt maturing within **strictly 1 year** (standard benchmark < 10%).\n\n### Tier 3: Practical Accounting, Procedures & Official Codal Forms\n• **Public Debt Register**, Maturity Profile Schedule, Debt Service Burden Sheet.\n\n### Tier 4: Statutory Exceptions, Restrictions & Competent Authorities\n• External borrowings can be contracted exclusively by Union Government under Article 292; States are prohibited from borrowing directly from foreign sources under Article 293.\n\n### Tier 5: SAS Examination Case Studies, Practical Calculations & Half-Margins\n• **Debt Analysis Case**: State Government's debt maturity profile shows 42% of total outstanding loans maturing within next 2 years.\n• **Audit Finding**: Severe debt rollover risk and vulnerability to debt distress reported in State Finance Report.\n\n---\n### Comprehensive Codal Study Notes & Statutory Analysis\n### Deficit Management through Internal & External Debt\n\n#### 1. Mechanics of Deficit Financing\nWhen total expenditure exceeds revenue receipts and non-debt capital receipts, the resultant Fiscal Deficit must be financed through sovereign borrowings. In India, deficit financing is managed through two primary channels:\n- **Internal Debt (Domestic Borrowings)**: Accounts for approximately **95%** of total sovereign public debt.\n- **External Debt (Foreign Sovereign Borrowings)**: Accounts for approximately **5%** of total debt.\n\n#### 2. Internal Debt Instruments & Mechanisms\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        INTERNAL DEBT FINANCING INSTRUMENTS                        |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| LONG-TERM INSTRUMENTS           | SHORT-TERM INSTRUMENTS      | LIQUIDITY BACKSTOP|\n| - Dated Government Securities   | - Treasury Bills (91, 182,  | - Ways and Means  |\n|   (G-Secs, 5 to 40-year tenure) |   364-day maturity)         |   Advances (WMA)  |\n| - Floating Rate Bonds (FRB)     | - Cash Management Bills     | - Special WMA     |\n| - Sovereign Green Bonds (SGrB)  |   (CMBs, < 91 days)         | - Overdraft (OD)  |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n1. **Dated Government Securities (G-Secs)**:\n   - Fixed-rate or floating-rate sovereign bonds issued by the Reserve Bank of India on behalf of the Government through periodic auctions on the **e-Kuber** platform. Tenures range from 5 years up to 40 years.\n   - Subscribed by commercial banks (to meet statutory SLR requirements), insurance companies (LIC), pension funds, and mutual funds.\n2. **Treasury Bills (T-Bills)**:\n   - Zero-coupon short-term debt instruments issued at a discount and redeemed at face value.\n   - Issued in three standard maturities: **91-day, 182-day, and 364-day**. Used to bridge short-term mismatches in receipts and expenditures.\n3. **Cash Management Bills (CMBs)**:\n   - Ultra-short-term instruments (maturities under 91 days) issued to meet temporary, unexpected cash crunches.\n4. **Ways and Means Advances (WMA) & Overdraft (OD) Facility**:\n   - Under Section 17(5) of the RBI Act 1934, RBI provides WMA advances to the Central and State Governments purely to bridge temporary receipt-payment timing mismatches.\n   - *Normal WMA*: Clean advance with interest charged at the RBI Repo Rate.\n   - *Special WMA*: Collateralized against the government's holding of G-Secs, charged at Repo Rate minus 1%.\n   - *Overdraft (OD)*: If WMA limits are exceeded, the government enters Overdraft, charged at Repo Rate plus 2%. Central overdraft cannot exceed 10 consecutive working days; State overdraft cannot exceed 14 consecutive working days.\n\n#### 3. External Debt Management\n- Sovereign external debt is raised primarily from multilateral institutions (World Bank, Asian Development Bank, Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank) and bilateral partners (Japan International Cooperation Agency - JICA, KfW Germany).\n- Borrowed almost exclusively on concessional terms for long-term infrastructure and social sector projects. Foreign commercial sovereign bond issuance in overseas currencies has been avoided to eliminate sovereign currency risk.",
            "gist": "Deficit management relies on Internal Debt (G-Secs, SDLs, T-Bills, NSSF) and External Debt (World Bank, ADB). Debt sustainability requires interest payments < 20% of revenue.",
            "crux": "1. States borrow internally via State Development Loans (SDLs) through RBI.\n2. Interest-to-Revenue ratio > 20% indicates debt stress.\n3. External debt is raised exclusively by the Central Government under Article 292.",
